<h2 id="experience">Experience</h2>

<h3 class="experience-header" id="amazon-web-services--8-years">Amazon Web Services — 8 years</h3>

<p>I build query languages, AI agent platforms, and developer tooling at AWS scale. Four threads run through my work: <strong>making complex systems accessible through language design</strong>, <strong>bringing AI agents from prototype to production</strong>, <strong>driving large-scale cost and reliability programs across org boundaries</strong>, and <strong>turning ambiguous multi-team problems into executable plans</strong>.</p>

<p class="role"><strong>AI Agent Platforms &amp; Open-Source Developer Tooling</strong> — CloudWatch (2024–present)</p>

<p>I proposed and led delivery of the <a href="https://github.com/awslabs/mcp">CloudWatch MCP Server</a> — an open-source Model Context Protocol integration that exposes CloudWatch APIs to AI agents and coding assistants. I authored the initial architecture and phased plan scoping MCP servers across all of CloudWatch, coordinated a Sr. PE, 2 PEs, and engineers across Seattle and Dublin to ship a unified server in 2 weeks, completed AppSec review in 2 days, and handled the final integration across the full repo for the AWSLabs release. The server has been downloaded 482,000+ times and drove CloudWatch Logs API adoption to 1.1 million calls from MCP clients, creating measurable indirect revenue impact within 6 months of launch.</p>

<p>I led the Odyssey AI Agents platform through its reInvent 2024 preview launch and into GA. I took technical ownership of DataHub — the data storage and compliance layer — inheriting it in red status and leading 2 engineers to deliver both Preview and GA on schedule. I established data compliance expertise with the Legal team and delivered a deletion pipeline in 4 days (scoped at 2–3 weeks) to meet legal requirements. I drove Project Hive, the sub-agent extensibility architecture that enables external teams (EKS, RDS, OpenSearch) to plug domain-specific agents into Odyssey; the EKS agent shipped to production using this pattern. I led my team to deliver Runbook Execution Agents in 3 weeks, personally fixing production bugs at ~4-hour turnaround. I designed Forge — a self-correcting autonomous pipeline where LLM agents take a feature specification and produce a shipped, reviewed code change with zero manual intervention, using a 5-phase state machine with async job queuing, crash-resilient agent sessions, and iterative self-repair from static analysis feedback. First end-to-end run delivered a production compiler feature in under 5 hours.</p>

<p class="role"><strong>Cost Optimization &amp; Operational Excellence</strong> — CloudWatch Logs (2024)</p>

<p>I owned the RobinWood cost optimization program end-to-end for CloudWatch Logs Frontend — defining scope, execution plan, and cross-team coordination with Kinesis, S3, and Finance. Out of $36.7M planned savings, the program captured $52.3M in projected annual savings and realized $36.2M YTD. I led 3 engineers across workstreams: Graviton migration ($10M+ savings), Kinesis throughput reduction ($4M+, requiring negotiation with S3 leadership on traffic patterns), and Timber log compression ($2M+, 26% reduction in compressed log size). I authored the S3 traffic consensus document reviewed by S3 directors, resolved the Kinesis billing ambiguity (shards vs. throughput), and deployed changes across all regions event-free.</p>

<p>I rolled out TLS 1.3 support across all CloudWatch Logs regions with zero customer impact — coordinating with the FAST2 security team for safe deployment. I authored two Corrections of Error presented at the AWS Ops meeting, created cross-team action items, and led weekly operational reviews driving availability improvements. I graduated as MCM Bridge Review after shadowing 37 MCMs.</p>

<p class="role"><strong>Observability Query Language &amp; Compiler Design</strong> — CloudWatch Logs (2022–present)</p>

<p>I own feature development for ScrollQL, the query compiler powering CloudWatch Logs Insights — the primary log analysis surface for millions of AWS customers. ScrollQL compiles a SQL-like query language into execution plans over distributed log storage; I work across the full compiler stack from parser extensions through AST rewrite rules to execution operators. I designed and shipped Lookup Tables end-to-end across 32 AWS regions — a query-time enrichment primitive that performs hash-join lookups against customer-provided reference data, enabling log enrichment without data movement. I build aggregation functions that operate over streaming window semantics, and macro rewrite rules that expand high-level query patterns into optimized multi-stage execution plans.</p>

<p class="role"><strong>Cross-Org Technical Leadership</strong> (2024–2025)</p>

<p>I led the Analytical Tier Benchmarking effort evaluating CloudWatch Logs Analytics Tier launch readiness across 9 benchmark iterations, producing the data that informed L7/L8 go/no-go decisions. I drove the 3P Data Source integration strategy for Odyssey — taking a multi-dimensional ambiguous problem (multiple providers, data types, backwards-compatible model changes) and proposing a phased plan (OTEL first, single provider, reuse connectors) approved by PEs. I led Knowledge Store region expansion with 3 engineers and authored tooling that onboarded all CWL services to AIOps investigations with under 5-minute setup. I drove AuthZ strategy for the Frontend, getting PE alignment on a phased approach to resource-level authorization. 506 code reviews sent in 2025, 680 in 2024, averaging 1.3 revisions per review. 26 hiring loops completed across two years.</p>

<p class="role"><strong>Infrastructure Compliance at Scale</strong> — AWS CloudFormation (2018–2022)</p>

<p>I designed and built the Hooks control plane service and led a team of 6 engineers to deliver CloudFormation Hooks to GA — a pre-provisioning compliance framework that intercepts stack operations and invokes customer-defined policy handlers before resources are created. When the senior engineer leading the project moved to another initiative, I stepped into the technical leadership role as a newly promoted SDE II — navigating leadership changes, team churn, and mid-flight architectural changes while simultaneously onboarding new engineers and making my own code contributions. I led the integration of Hooks into the CloudFormation Registry and drove both systems across all AWS partitions (commercial, GovCloud, China, isolated regions). Peers described the role as <em>“acting as Hooks manager while also performing engineer duty”</em> and <em>“rising to any challenge — leading the engineering team through internal beta, external beta, and GA.”</em> Rated “Exceeds High Bar” in both 2022 and 2023. When Hooks grew into a dedicated Policy-as-Code team, I established the sprint process and knowledge-sharing mechanisms that set the team up for continued delivery after my transition to CloudWatch Logs.</p>

<p>After transitioning to CloudWatch Logs, I ramped on the Uluru query execution stack and became the go-to engineer for Uluru-related issues within months — leading an S-team goal, root-causing production incidents (JDK17 dependency failures, WLP certificate bugs, reInvent deployment blockers), and initiating the migration from the monolithic CloudWatch frontend to micro-frontends. Rated “Exceeds High Bar” in 2023 with Ownership, Dive Deep, and Deliver Results as top peer-recognized strengths.</p>

